ID: B2-Ch11c-Herbalist
TEXT:

 "Rose, listen to me," I say, stepping into her line of sight. "Arraka showed me your ritual. I know how brutal it is, but you need to--"

"It's not enough," Rose says. Her gaze stays fixed on empty air.

"What?"

"Barry, it's not enough." She finally turns her eyes to mine, her voice raw. "Nothing I have done in my entire life comes close to enough. I can't keep hiding behind being powerless. I can't live like that anymore. I have to do more."

"Do more for who? The slaves in Thilias?"

"Not just the slaves," she says. "Everyone I ever looked away from because I told myself I couldn't change anything. I am done closing my eyes to it. I want to help them all."

#if(v_ch11_saved_slave != 3) {
 "Rose," Daren says gently. "That's noble, but good intentions alone don't keep people alive. You already do more than your share gathering herbs and treating the sick across Thilias. You took Ella in knowing the danger. You can't shoulder the impossible, and nobody expects you to march off and right every wrong in the world. You aren't a mage. You don't have the power to alter things on that scale."

"Don't I?" Rose asks. "Look around us. This frozen world—did I not do this?"

"That's the prophecy taking hold," Daren says. "You've never had conscious control over--"

Rose vanishes mid-sentence. She reappears a dozen feet away, a fierce, triumphant smile on her face.

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_slave == 3) {
 "Rose," Daren says gently. "That's noble, but good intentions alone don't keep people alive. You already do more than your share gathering herbs and treating the sick across Thilias. You can't shoulder the impossible, and nobody expects you to march off and right every wrong in the world. You aren't a mage. You don't have the power to alter things on that scale."

"Don't I?" Rose asks. "Look around us. This frozen world—did I not do this?"

"That's the prophecy taking hold," Daren says. "You've never had conscious control over--"

Rose vanishes mid-sentence. She reappears a dozen feet away, a fierce, triumphant smile on her face.

}
 "Not bad, herbalist," Melindra says with an appreciative smirk. "Not bad at all."

"Rose, that was incredible," Kate says, the exhaustion on her face lifting for a second. "How did you do that?"

"I don't know," Rose says, pressing a hand to her temple. "Everything feels different. Like my head is flooded with information I can barely sort through. Most of the time magic is still out of reach, but the rest feels completely natural right now."

"Hah!" Arraka cackles from the amulet. "Don't flatter yourself, herbalist. You're riding the Magium link. The moment this prophecy wraps up, the link cuts, you forget every scrap of it, and you go right back to being the same boring, helpless mortal you were before."

"Then I'll learn it all from scratch," Rose says, her jaw set. "Day and night until I have it down. I refuse to go back to how I was. This time I'm making a real difference."

choice("Encourage Rose in her decision", B2-Ch11c-Encourage, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Encourage, v_b2_ch11_encourage = 1)
choice("Try to convince Rose that her plan is too dangerous", B2-Ch11c-Encourage, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Encourage, v_b2_ch11_encourage = 0)
choice("Tell Rose that her plan sounds stupid", B2-Ch11c-Encourage, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Encourage, v_b2_ch11_encourage = 2)


ID: B2-Ch11c-Encourage
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b2_ch11_supportive,1) if (v_b2_ch11_encourage == 1)
achievement("Supportive friend",v_ac_b2_ch11_supportive)
#if(v_b2_ch11_encourage == 1) {
 "Ignore Arraka," I tell Rose. "Put in the work and you'll master it in no time."

"Thank you, Barry." Rose smiles. "I'll make sure none of you regret backing me."

"Hate to break up the sentiment," Melindra says, "but didn't you mention sabotaging this ritual? If you have a scheme, move on it. We have no idea when stage two drops."

"She's right," Daren says. "Rose, can you use your powers to unfreeze the world around us? Breaking the stasis might be enough to unravel the ritual."

"I don't know if it'll work, but I'll try," Rose says.

She shuts her eyes and breathes in deep. The clearing goes dead quiet to give her room. A breath later, she blinks out of existence and snaps into view five feet to the left. She vanishes and reappears three more times in rapid hops, popping across the grass before Daren finally speaks up.

}
#if(v_b2_ch11_encourage == 0) {
 "You need to think this through before throwing yourself down that path," I tell Rose. "Even if you master time magic, marching into danger like that will get you killed."

"I was hoping you'd stand with me, Barry," Rose says, her smile dimming. "Still, I appreciate you looking out for me. Don't worry. I'm not going to be reckless."

"Hate to break up the sentiment," Melindra says, "but didn't you mention sabotaging this ritual? If you have a scheme, move on it. We have no idea when stage two drops."

"She's right," Daren says. "Rose, can you use your powers to unfreeze the world around us? Breaking the stasis might be enough to unravel the ritual."

"I don't know if it'll work, but I'll try," Rose says.

She shuts her eyes and breathes in deep. The clearing goes dead quiet to give her room. A breath later, she blinks out of existence and snaps into view five feet to the left. She vanishes and reappears three more times in rapid hops, popping across the grass before Daren finally speaks up.

}
#if(v_b2_ch11_encourage == 2) {
 "It sounds like a terrible idea to me," I say. "Are you sure you want to charge headfirst into something this dangerous?"

"Of all the people here, you're the last one I expected a lecture on recklessness from, Barry," Rose says. "My mind is made up. Even if it takes years, I'll learn to control this power and put it toward fixing what's broken."

"Hate to break up the sentiment," Melindra says, "but didn't you mention sabotaging this ritual? If you have a scheme, move on it. We have no idea when stage two drops."

"She's right," Daren says. "Rose, can you use your powers to unfreeze the world around us? Breaking the stasis might be enough to unravel the ritual."

"I don't know if it'll work, but I'll try," Rose says.

She shuts her eyes and breathes in deep. The clearing goes dead quiet to give her room. A breath later, she blinks out of existence and snaps into view five feet to the left. She vanishes and reappears three more times in rapid hops, popping across the grass before Daren finally speaks up.

}
 "Rose, what's happening?" Daren asks.

"I'm trying to test the boundaries," Rose says. "I can freeze you all whenever I want, and the hold doesn't slip no matter how far I walk. I can release you at will too. But whenever I try to release the rest of the world, nothing moves. It's like the world's stasis runs on an entirely different lock than mine."

"It was cast with your power," Melindra says. "You just didn't do it consciously, so you can't manually undo it. The world locked so you could deliver the prophecy. Don't you remember what happened after you went into the trance?"

"Bits and pieces," Rose murmurs. "The moment the trance took hold, time froze for everyone on the continent, including you. Then I walked for days without stopping until I reached this clearing. Once I was in front of you, I unfroze our group so I could speak."

"Exactly," Melindra says. "Standard prophecy mechanics. The God of Time found a way to hijack the connection for his own trials, but that doesn't hand you the key to drop the entire continental freeze."

"Hold on," Arraka interrupts. "Rose, did you just say this was the first group you visited after entering the trance?"

"Yes, I'm certain of it," Rose says. "Why?"

"Ahahahahaha!" Arraka's laughter erupts from the amulet, shrill and harsh. "You're telling me these chumps are the highest-priority actors for the prophecy? Pack your bags, everyone! This continent is doomed!"

"What is she talking about?" Daren asks Melindra over Arraka's shrieks. "Why would we be first? Are there other groups getting this prophecy?"

"Yes," Melindra says. "You're only the first stop. Once the God of Time finishes his stage, you lock back into stasis while the time weaver visits the rest. On top of that, this same prophecy will be delivered to you and the other recipients at least two more times before the catastrophe hits, updating everyone on whether any progress has been made toward averting it. Time weavers always visit targets in order of priority. Your group carries the heaviest weight in deciding how the future plays out."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11c-Practice, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Practice)


ID: B2-Ch11c-Practice
TEXT:

 "Great," Daren mutters. "I don't know whether to take comfort in our odds or dread being ground zero when it hits. Either way, standing here won't help. We need to find a way to disrupt this ritual. If Rose can't break the freeze, we look for physical seams. Hadrik, come with me. Let's sweep the perimeter for anything out of place."

"Right behind you," Hadrik says, stepping into stride.

"Did you really walk all those days without stopping?" Flower asks Rose, eyes wide with concern as Daren and Hadrik move off into the woods. "Aren't you starving? Don't your legs hurt?"

"No, I feel fine," Rose says. "Not hungry, not tired. In fact, I feel just as rested as the morning I left my house in Thilias. Is it the trance?"

"Of course it's the trance," Arraka says. "You were held in total magical stasis for the march. No need for food, water, or sleep. If the God of Time hadn't dragged you out early to torment these losers, you wouldn't remember a single step of the walk."

"I see," Rose says quietly. She hesitates, glancing down at the amulet. "Arraka... do time weavers have other abilities besides freezing and rewinding time?"

"Looking for a training session, are we?" Arraka sneers. "You should be able to slow time down around yourself. To anyone watching, you'd look like a blur. The perk is that you can still touch and manipulate things while moving through a slowed field. You might even be able to un-slow people nearby so they move at your pace, though I can't guarantee whether that's an innate weaver trait or a high-tier spell. Aside from that, nothing comes to mind."

"Thank you!" Rose says. "Rewinding time doesn't seem to respond at all, but slowing it down might work. Let me try."

Rose gets to work testing her focus, with Kate and Flower offering steady pointers. Daren and Hadrik circle the frozen treeline, poking at leaves and bark in search of magical fractures.

Leila stands well apart from the rest of us at the clearing's edge. She hasn't uttered a written word or moved from her spot since the trials ended, eyes glued to the dirt.

Her trial must have hit just as hard as Kate's. She grew up in the Beacon too, which means her loop probably dragged her through the same nightmare. Kate has years of built-up armor to weather that kind of trauma, but Leila carries hers raw.

I walk over to her. "Leila, are you alright?"

She starts at my voice, looking up in surprise. She's far too used to fading into the background.

She meets my eyes and gives a small, careful nod.

"I'm fine," glowing blue letters form in the air before her. "Just a little shaken."

"Do you want to talk about it?"

Leila hesitates, then offers a faint, fragile smile as new words hang between us.

"Not now. But maybe later..."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11c-Slowing, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Slowing)


ID: B2-Ch11c-Slowing
TEXT:

 I give Leila some space to collect herself and walk back toward the center of the clearing.

Flower is chatting with Melindra near the roots of an oak, while Kate stays by Rose's side. Rose has already figured out how to slow down her own temporal field and is now trying to pull Kate into the acceleration with her. I join in to help gauge the field, but after fifteen minutes of trial and error, the linkage refuses to hold.

"It's been a while since the first trials wrapped up," Daren notes, pacing back in from the trees. "Why is the second stage taking so long to trigger?"

"Because you idiots fed Selkram a feast," Arraka says. "He's still busy refining the haul into raw power."

"What haul?" Daren asks.

"Your misery!" Arraka snaps. "Negative emotion is the raw fuel Selkram harvests to convert into magical energy. While you losers were busy looping through your worst nightmares, drowning in despair and screaming curses at the sky, you filled his reserves to the brim. Honestly, you gave him so much juice he might not even need a second stage from us. We'll find out soon enough."

#if(v_ch4_spared_dave == 1) {
 "Glad to be of service," Daren says dryly. "That gives us more time to search the perimeter. Come on, Hadrik. Let's test more of these frozen insects and branches. Maybe one isn't wrapped in an impenetrable ward, and we can crack this stasis from the outside."

Daren and Hadrik resume their sweep, but before they make it thirty paces, a shrill, panicked shriek tears through the trees a hundred feet away.

We whip around toward the commotion. Cutthroat Dave is stumbling backward through the underbrush, drenched in sweat, eyes darting in blind terror as he tries to make sense of the frozen woods. The invisible stalker Arraka and Selkram mentioned earlier—with everything that went down during the prophecy, I had completely forgotten about him.

"Well, look who finally dropped his cover," Melindra says with a smirk.

She flicks her wrist, and a spiraling updraft catches Dave by the belt, hoisting him kicking into the air.

"Why don't you join the party so we can get a proper look at you?"

The wind drags him toward us in steady increments while he thrashes against the invisible grip.

"Put me down, you witch!" Dave roars, hurling three throwing daggers straight at her face.

Melindra raises a casual gust that knocks the blades aside mid-flight, reeling him in without missing a beat.

}
#if(v_ch4_spared_dave == 0) {
 "Glad to be of service," Daren says dryly. "That gives us more time to search the perimeter. Come on, Hadrik. Let's test more of these frozen insects and branches. Maybe one isn't wrapped in an impenetrable ward, and we can crack this stasis from the outside."

Daren and Hadrik spend the next few minutes scouring the frozen foliage, but find nothing that yields to force.

A blinding white flash washes over the clearing. The pull hits my physical frame this time—not just my consciousness, but flesh and bone wrenched across space.

When the glare recedes, the forest is gone. We stand in the center of an impossible metropolis.

Four colossal black towers dominate each corner of the city skyline, their sheer surfaces gleaming with the matte dark sheen of seredium dust. Atop each spire, a violent vortex of purple magical energy churns wildly against invisible containment, bright enough to sear the eyes. Massive platforms glide up and down the flanks of the thousand-foot towers, shuttling hundreds of people between elevated levels.

Above our heads, hundreds of horseless metal carriages streak through the sky in frantic transit. Towering holographic screens hover over the avenues, projecting the looping broadcast of an announcer shouting in Common: 'All non-lessathi citizens are to present themselves to the military reserve area immediately. We are under attack. I repeat, we are under attack!'

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch11c-Jewelry, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Jewelry, v_b2_ch11_echoes = 0) if (v_ch4_spared_dave == 1)
choice("Continue", B2-Ch11c-Frenzy, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Frenzy, v_b2_ch11_echoes = 0) if (v_ch4_spared_dave == 0)


ID: B2-Ch11c-Hesitation
TEXT:

 "Don't you dare speak for Rose, you undead parasite," Daren snarls, his grip white on his hilt. "Who are you to say what she would have wanted? You stole her corpse, pledged yourself to a monster, and left her little brother and sister weeping at home over an empty grave. I don't care if you stole her face or her memories. You are nothing but hollowed-out meat. You will never be Rose. Never."

The revenant flinches. The blank stillness in her eyes cracks wide open, her hands trembling as she stares at him.

"Did... did Suzie and Kevin really cry when they found out—"

"Do not speak their names!" Daren roars, stepping closer. "You aren't Rose! If you had a shred of decency in you, you'd vacate that stolen body so we could carry her home and let those children say a proper goodbye!"

"I cannot do that," the revenant says, the flat mask settling over her features again. "I exist to serve the God of Death. Only my master may end my life. No one else."

"Then you're our enemy," Daren says, his voice dropping low and cold. "We've seen what Death's zealots do. We have zero reason to treat you any differently than Selkram. Trusting you would be suicide."

"The God of Death has no quarrel with your group," she says evenly.

"And if he did? If he ordered you to slit our throats right here, would you?"

"Without hesitation," the revenant says, her expression completely unchanged. "It is not my place to question my master's commands."

"Then you don't leave this clearing alive," Daren says, drawing his longsword. "I'm ending this before you turn on us."

"Daren, stop!" Kate steps between them, arms raised. "You can't kill her. A piece of Rose is still in there—you saw her face when you mentioned the children. There has to be a way to reach her. We can--"

"Rose is gone, Kate," Daren cuts her off, relentless. "Stop clinging to a ghost. Let her rest. It's what Rose would want if she were here."

"Rose would never tell you to slaughter what's left of her!" Kate snaps. "She would want us working together, and you know it!"

"Sorry, Kate," Hadrik says, stepping up beside Daren. "I'm with the healer on this one. We have no idea what Death is cooking up. For all we know, he's in league with Time, running a double game on our heads. We put her down before she sticks a knife in our backs."

choice("Place myself between the revenant and Daren, in order to defend her", B2-Ch11c-Defend, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Defend, v_b2_ch11_defend = 1)
choice("Let Daren and Hadrik handle the situation", B2-Ch11c-Defend, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Defend, v_b2_ch11_defend = 0)


ID: B2-Ch11c-Defend
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch11_defend == 1) {
 Daren takes two strides toward the revenant, sword raised. I step directly into his path, slipping my dagger from its sheath.

"Barry, what are you doing?" Daren halts, scowling down at me.

"What does it look like?"

"We aren't doing this again," Daren says. "This isn't Illuna. The creature just admitted she'll butcher us the moment her god gives the order. Why are you standing in front of her?"

"Only if her god gives the order," I say. "Right now we need time magic for whatever trials Selkram drops on us next. Especially if stage two forces group coordination."

"All the more reason to put her down now!" Daren snaps. "What stops her from gutting us mid-trial? Even if she holds back today, this is our only chance to face her alone. What happens when she returns with a squad of death mages backing her?"

"And if you fail to kill her?" I counter. "Attacking an emissary unprovoked is declaring war on the God of Death. Do you want two gods actively trying to erase us?"

"We won't fail if we strike together," Daren says.

"Daren has the right of it," Hadrik says, closing in from the flank. "Every spell has a fracture point. We'll find hers."

A sheet of jagged ice bursts from the turf, five paces wide, walling Daren and Hadrik off from the revenant and me.

"Everyone take a breath before you do something irreversible," Kate's voice cuts through the chill, cold and flat.

"I appreciate the intervention," the revenant says, stepping calmly around the ice barrier, "but it is unnecessary. Daren and Hadrik need to experience the futility of their assault before they will cooperate. Do not interfere. I will engage them both."

"Big talk," Hadrik grins. "Let's see the muscle behind it."

}
#if(v_b2_ch11_defend == 0) {
 I keep my dagger sheathed and let Daren and Hadrik take the lead.

"Don't take another step toward her," Kate warns, her voice tightening as Daren advances.

Daren pauses, turning slightly. "You want me to stand down? Treat her like a trusted ally? Not after what she told us."

"I'm saying we don't strike unprovoked," Kate says. "Antagonizing a god when we can barely survive the one we have is madness."

"This might be our only window while she's isolated!" Daren argues. "What if she returns with an army of undead hunters at her back? We can't afford the gamble."

"Daren is right," Hadrik says, stepping up to Daren's shoulder. "If we're fighting, we fight now. Every spell has a limit. We'll find hers."

A sheet of jagged ice bursts from the turf, walling Daren and Hadrik off from the revenant.

"Everyone take a breath before you do something irreversible," Kate says, her voice cold and flat.

"Kate, I appreciate the intervention," the revenant says, stepping calmly around the ice barrier, "but it is unnecessary. Daren and Hadrik need to experience the futility of their assault before they will cooperate. Do not interfere. I will engage them both."

"Big talk," Hadrik grins. "Let's see the muscle behind it."

}
 Hadrik lunges forward, throwing a heavy punch aimed at her jaw. The strike cuts through empty air—the revenant vanishes before the knuckles land and reappears ten paces back, expression completely flat. Hadrik drives forward again, swinging hard, with the exact same result. Daren sweeps in with a low sword arc; she disappears and materializes behind him with effortless stillness.

"What is this?" Hadrik pants, planting his boots. "How are you slipping away? Time is already frozen!"

"It is frozen for the rest of the world, not for you," the revenant says. "I simply drop you into the world's stasis for a heartbeat and step aside before the blow lands. And the mechanic functions just as well on offense."

She blinks out of sight. A split-second later she is standing behind Daren, a steel blade pressed flush against the bare flesh of his throat.

"Do you yield, healer, or do we continue?"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11c-Asleep, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Asleep)


ID: B2-Ch11c-Asleep
TEXT:

 "Nice bluff, revenant," Daren says. He drops his sword into its scabbard, reaches up with both hands, and clamps her knife wrist, hauling the steel flush against his throat. "Your knife lacks the enchantments to bite through even the basic kinetic ward I carry."

The revenant strains against his grip, but Daren's hold is iron.

"And since you haven't vanished, my read was right: you cannot teleport out of a physical hold with time-freeze. Nor can you rewind time at will, or you'd have undone this already."

Daren twists, heaving her across his hip, and slams her into the grass with his full weight pinning her down.

"Which leaves you completely exposed."

He clears his scabbard with his right hand and severs her head in one clean stroke.

"Daren..." Kate's voice breaks. "What did you do?..."

"What had to be--"

A violent jolt scrambles my senses. The clearing blurs, snapping like waking from a dead sleep.

The corpse is gone. The revenant stands behind Daren with the dagger at his throat, exactly as before—except this time she leaps backward the instant his fingers twitch, putting three paces of turf between them.

Did I blackout mid-fight? A mind spell? No—the physical reality is solid, but the memory of her decapitation hangs in my head like a phantom limb.

"No..." Daren stumbles back, his sword arm trembling as he points at her. "You were dead! I severed your neck! It reversed—just like when Arraka broke from the amulet! Who rewound the field?!"

A localized time reversal. It caught only Daren and the revenant in its immediate radius, leaving the rest of us with only the ghost of an unmade reality.

"I did not cast it," the revenant says, her voice steady and flat. "Time weavers possess an innate safeguard against death until the prophecy is fulfilled. Kill me as often as you like; time will rewind to the moment before the fatal error so I may escape. Your assault is entirely futile."

"Liar!" Daren shouts. "If that's true, why didn't time rewind to save Rose? Why didn't she survive?"

"Because of the necromantic binding placed upon her before she fell," the revenant answers. "The Magium requires only a viable consciousness to carry the prophecy forward. Since my soul was already bound to occupy her vessel upon her death, the rewind mechanism saw no disruption. I became the weaver in her place."

"So... Rose would still be alive if not for you?..." Daren asks, his voice hollow.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11c-Paranoid, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Paranoid)


ID: B2-Ch11c-Paranoid
TEXT:

 "Daren..." Kate steps between them, her voice tight. "It wasn't her doing. Zack cast that curse. She didn't choose this vessel or ask to serve the God of Death. Blaming her won't bring Rose back."

She turns to the revenant, reaching out to clasp her hands gently.

"Listen to me. I don't know what to call you. 'Revenant' is cruel, and you aren't the Rose I knew. But if any piece of her survives in you, come with us. Walk away from Death. We can find a way to hide you, break the binding, fight him—anything. You don't have to carry this alone."

The revenant pulls her hands away, stepping back. For a heartbeat, deep grief shadows her eyes before vanishing behind the mask.

"I am sorry. I am not who you wish me to be. For your own survival, keep your distance. I will not hesitate to strike you down if my master commands it. My obedience to the God of Death is absolute. Putting your faith in me will only get you killed. That is the remnant of Rose warning you to stay away. Do you understand?"

"Yes," Kate says softly, stepping back. "I understand. Thank you for the warning."

"So where does that leave us?" Hadrik asks. "Working side by side like we didn't just try to take each other's heads off?"

"It seems we have no alternative," Daren says, reluctantly sheathing his sword.

"What about the God of Death?" Melindra asks, crossing her arms. "What story are you taking back to him, revenant?"

"If he inquires, the truth," the revenant says. "I will inform him that I initiated the confrontation to test your capabilities. He will have no reason to direct hostility toward your group."

"Right," Melindra says.

"Though I doubt he will ask," the revenant adds. "From his perspective, no time has elapsed. He will likely continue ignoring me inside his temple until he requires an errand."

"He ignores you?" Kate asks. "Why?"

"The God of Death does not trust me," the revenant says. "He believes I am a plant sent by the God of Time to infiltrate his priesthood. He attempted to execute me several times, gave up when the rewind triggered, and confined me to the temple without access to his strategy to prevent leaks to Selkram."

Arraka's laughter rings from the amulet.

"Classic Ulruk!" she cackles. "Paranoid coward has always been terrified of Time. I can only imagine his shrieks when a time weaver landed on his doorstep. Settle in, revenant—you'll be sitting on your hands in that temple for a long time."

choice(""Why is Ulruk so suspicious, when it comes to the God of Time?"", B2-Ch11c-Channel, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Channel, v_b2_ch11_paranoid = 0)
choice(""Can't Ulruk just read the revenant's aura, to see if she's lying, like Eiden does?"", B2-Ch11c-Channel, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Channel, v_b2_ch11_paranoid = 1)
choice(""How did the revenant manage to come here, if she was locked inside a temple?"", B2-Ch11c-Channel, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Channel, v_b2_ch11_paranoid = 2)


ID: B2-Ch11c-Channel
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch11_paranoid == 0) {
 "Why is Ulruk so suspicious, when it comes to the God of Time?"

"Those two have never gotten along," Arraka says. "Selkram is the only god without a single worshipper to his name, so Ulruk is convinced Time's grand scheme is to murder him and hijack his flock. Selkram is far too arrogant for that, but Death is an absolute lunatic, just like the cultists who pray to him. Nobody can talk him out of it."

"We're burning daylight," Melindra cuts in. "Revenant, you mentioned sabotaging the next trial. What's the play?"

}
#if(v_b2_ch11_paranoid == 1) {
 "Can't Ulruk just read the revenant's aura, to see if she's lying, like Eiden does?"

"Good luck reading the aura of a corpse," Arraka says. "She's undead. Checking her flow for deceit is like reading a brick. There's barely enough circulation to register. You might catch a ripple during rare spikes of emotion, but I wouldn't bet a copper on it."

"We're burning daylight," Melindra cuts in. "Revenant, you mentioned sabotaging the next trial. What's the play?"

}
#if(v_b2_ch11_paranoid == 2) {
 "How did the revenant manage to come here, if she was locked inside a temple?"

"That glowing-eyed stasis lets her phase straight through solid stone, steel, and flesh," Arraka says. "It would be pathetic if the Magium gave her a failsafe to revive from death but couldn't bypass a locked door. Prophecies don't get stopped by a latch."

"We're burning daylight," Melindra cuts in. "Revenant, you mentioned sabotaging the next trial. What's the play?"

}
#if(v_b2_ch9_channel == 0) {
 "There is a method to pull hints about what lies ahead in the next trial," the revenant says. "The simplest route would have been utilizing Barry's hearing stat, but that is no longer an option."

"Why not?" I ask.

"Because you refused to keep the transceiver channel open," she says. "There was a precise action required right before the stasis took hold, but I could not explain it over an open link without alerting Death to our coordination. Regardless, the window closed. There is another method to extract the necessary intel, though it is far less reliable. Does anyone in this group possess acute magical senses?"

}
#if(v_b2_ch6_stat == 0 && v_b2_ch9_channel == 1) {
 "There is a method to pull hints about what lies ahead in the next trial," the revenant says. "The simplest route is utilizing Barry's hearing stat, but I could not explain the specifics over the transceiver without risking Death discovering our coordination. I do not know how much of my instruction was clear. Barry, did you enter your stat device's secret override code before time froze?"

"No," I say. "I don't know any override codes for the device. I didn't know they existed."

"I see," the revenant says. "I had hoped your research into lessathi devices would have uncovered the sequence. Had you entered it prior to the freeze, your hearing boost would have held for the duration of the stasis. As it stands, we must rely on our secondary option. Does anyone in this group possess acute magical senses?"

}
#if(v_b2_ch10_activate == 0 && v_b2_ch6_stat == 1 && v_b2_ch9_channel == 1) {
 "There is a method to pull hints about what lies ahead in the next trial," the revenant says. "The simplest route is utilizing Barry's hearing stat, but I could not explain the specifics over the transceiver without risking Death discovering our coordination. I do not know how much of my instruction was clear. Barry, did you enter your stat device's secret override code before time froze?"

"No," I say. "You gave me zero context, and I wasn't walking blind into a potential trap. Can I enter it now?"

"No," the revenant says. "Inputting the sequence now will only take effect once the stasis drops, rendering it useless to us. The temporary boost relies on an internal magical timer that remains dormant as long as the world is frozen. Had you triggered it seconds before the freeze, the countdown would have locked alongside the world, granting you heightened perception for the entire duration of this stasis."

"So time hadn't frozen yet when you called?"

"No," she says. "A series of uncontrolled localized freezes hit me just before the trance locked in, warning me that the continental freeze was seconds away. The same precursor occurred before my previous transmission, though that was only an initial pulse. That is why I instructed you to keep the channel open so I could cue the code at the exact threshold. Since that opportunity passed, we must pivot to our secondary option. Does anyone in this group possess acute magical senses?"

As soon as she finishes, all eyes turn to Arraka's amulet.

"Arraka, will you--" Illuna begins.

"Yeah, yeah, I've got it," Arraka grumbles. "What's the drill?"

"You need to position yourself as close as possible to the point where the God of Time first manifested," the revenant says.

"I'm on it!" Flower says, kicking off the turf as her fire jets ignite.

She streaks upward to the elevation where Selkram hovered and halts, hovering on plumes of flame.

"I'm in position," Flower calls down. "Now what?"

}
