ID: B2-Ch11c-Enough
TEXT:

 "Yes, exactly," the revenant says. "The enchantment that brought me into this world was designed to implant extensive knowledge from the God of Death into my mind, including the mechanics of lessathi stat devices. In short, the temporary boost triggered by your override code relies on an internal timer that does not elapse while the world is held in stasis. Because you entered it prior to the freeze, the countdown locked in place, granting you heightened perception for the duration of the spell."

"So time hadn't frozen yet when you called?"

"No," she says. "Precursor freezes occurred moments before the trance, warning me that the full continental lock was imminent. That was why I instructed you to keep the channel open. Having the code active is only the first requirement, however. To perceive the temporal fracture, you need four or more points invested in Hearing, bringing your boosted total to level seven. Do you have the requisite stats?"

"Yeah, I have Hearing maxed," I say. "What am I listening for?"

"First, position yourself as close as possible to the point where the God of Time first manifested," the revenant says.

"Right. Melindra, can you--"

Before I finish the sentence, Melindra's air currents gather beneath my boots, lifting me smoothly into the air.

"Thanks," I say, leaning into the updraft.

I rise to the elevation where Selkram hovered and hang suspended in the wind.

"I'm in position," I call down. "Now what?"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11c-Enough2, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Enough2)


ID: B2-Ch11c-Maximum
TEXT:

 "Yes, exactly," the revenant says. "The enchantment that brought me into this world was designed to implant extensive knowledge from the God of Death into my mind, including the mechanics of lessathi stat devices. In short, the temporary boost triggered by your override code relies on an internal timer that does not elapse while the world is held in stasis. Because you entered it prior to the freeze, the countdown locked in place, granting you heightened perception for the duration of the spell."

"So time hadn't frozen yet when you called?"

"No," she says. "Precursor freezes occurred moments before the trance, warning me that the full continental lock was imminent. That was why I instructed you to keep the channel open. Having the code active is only the first requirement, however. To perceive the temporal fracture, you need four or more points invested in Hearing, bringing your boosted total to level seven. Do you have the requisite stats?"

"No," I say. "Four is my current stat ceiling, and I haven't maxed Hearing yet."

"If your baseline ceiling is four," the revenant says, "the only way to surpass it is by applying the code on top of four existing points. The device will not allow you to allocate additional points past the cap manually. We must proceed with the secondary approach. Does anyone in this group possess acute magical senses?"

All eyes turn to Arraka's amulet.

"Arraka, will you--" Illuna begins.

"Yeah, yeah, I've got it," Arraka grumbles. "What's the drill?"

"You need to position yourself as close as possible to the point where the God of Time first manifested," the revenant says.

"I'm on it!" Flower says, kicking off the turf as her fire jets ignite.

She streaks upward to the elevation where Selkram hovered and halts, hovering on plumes of flame.

"I'm in position," Flower calls down. "Now what?"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11c-Maximum2, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Maximum2)


ID: B2-Ch11c-Enough2
TEXT:

 "Focus your attention on the air directly before you," the revenant calls up. "Because this is the exact threshold where the God of Time anchored his Magium link, an invisible temporal fracture remains. Future voices leak through the rupture. You will need to filter out ambient noise entirely to isolate the echoes. Can you do it?"

"I'll try," I say.

"Take your time. We have a window before the next stage initiates."

I shut my eyes, blocking out the wind beneath me and the voices on the ground, narrowing my focus to the empty pocket of space where Selkram stood.

At first there is only a faint, hollow hum. Then layered murmurs bleed through—dozens of voices overlapping in rapid succession. I push harder, filtering the resonance until individual words separate. I catch fragments of dialogue, but the strain drives a spike of white-hot pressure through my skull, forcing me to break focus.

"Is something wrong?" the revenant asks as I press the heels of my palms to my temples.

"Yeah," I breathe, blinking through the throbbing ache. "Sudden headache."

"If the concentration caused physical strain, do not push further," she says. "We cannot afford you collapsing before the trial starts. Did you extract anything actionable?"

"An unfamiliar woman's voice," I say, lowering my hands. "She was gloating about casting a spell on you to prevent you from focusing on time-freezes. That's all I pulled before the pressure hit."

"A spell targeting concentration?" the revenant muses. "A dizziness curse, most likely. Does anyone present know a counter-ward against dizziness?"

"I do," Illuna says. "Though the ward carries a vulnerability to suggestion magic. Do you accept the trade-off?"

"If it is the only ward available, yes," the revenant says. "I prefer mitigating a confirmed hazard over fearing an unconfirmed one."

"Very well," Illuna says.

She raises her hands, chanting a low incantation. White light wraps around the revenant for three heartbeats before sinking into her skin. Melindra lets the air currents subside, lowering me back to the grass.

"The ward is set," Illuna says.

"A small edge," Daren mutters, "but I was hoping for something that could shut down the trial entirely. Revenant, can you attempt to unfreeze the world and collapse the ritual?"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11c-Apologized, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Apologized, v_b2_ch11_echoes = 1)


ID: B2-Ch11c-Maximum2
TEXT:

 "Arraka must extend her magical sense into the space where Selkram anchored his Magium link," the revenant says. "An invisible temporal fracture lingers there, leaking echoes of future voices. If Arraka inspects the rift with sufficient precision, she may be able to discern what is being spoken, even without physical hearing."

"Right, I sense the tear," Arraka's voice floats down. "And there are definitely echoes leaking through. But it's a tangled mess of overlapping noise. Give me a second."

We wait in silence, watching Flower hover on twin columns of fire. After thirty seconds, Arraka clicks her tongue in disgust.

"Forget it. Nothing coherent is coming through that mess. If it were two or three distinct threads, maybe, but it's a chaotic wall of sound and too faint to parse with magical sense alone."

"Are you certain?" the revenant asks. "You have time to attempt another scan."

"I'm certain. It's white noise," Arraka snaps. "If you want to sabotage the ritual, find a method that actually works."

"Can we stop the trial from triggering altogether?" Daren asks as Flower descends back to the grass. "Can't you unfreeze the world around us and collapse the ritual from the inside?"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11c-Apologized, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Apologized, v_b2_ch11_echoes = 0)


ID: B2-Ch11c-Apologized
TEXT:

 "The continental freeze occurred without my conscious command, so I cannot undo it," the revenant says. "Time froze across the land long before I appeared before you. Up until my arrival, your party was held in stasis along with the rest of the continent while I walked in the trance. Time unfroze for you only when I drew close enough to speak the prophecy."

"Wait a second," Arraka's voice crackles from the amulet. "You're saying this is the first group you visited since entering the trance?"

"Yes, I am certain of it," the revenant says. "Why?"

"Ahahahahaha!" Arraka howls with laughter. "You're telling me these chumps are the highest-priority actors on the continent? Pack your bags, everyone! We're doomed!"

"What is she shrieking about?" Daren asks Melindra over the din. "Why would we be first? Are other groups receiving this prophecy?"

"Yes," Melindra says. "You're only the first stop. Once the God of Time finishes his stage, you lock back into stasis while the time weaver visits the rest. On top of that, this same prophecy will be delivered to you and the other recipients at least two more times before the catastrophe hits, updating everyone on whether any progress has been made toward averting it. Time weavers always visit targets in order of priority. Your group carries the heaviest weight in deciding how the future plays out."

"Great," Daren mutters. "I don't know whether to take comfort in our odds or dread being ground zero when it hits. Either way, standing here won't help. We need to find a way to disrupt this ritual. If the revenant has no other angles, we look for physical seams. Hadrik, come with me. Let's sweep the perimeter for anything out of place."

"Right behind you," Hadrik says.

"Did you really walk all those days without stopping?" Flower asks the revenant, her eyes wide with worry as Daren and Hadrik move off. "Aren't you starving? Don't your legs hurt?"

"I am undead, so I require neither food nor rest," the revenant says, her tone completely flat. "Even were that not the case, the trance holds the body in complete magical stasis, suspending physical decay and fatigue until the duty concludes. If the God of Time had not intervened, I would not even remember the journey."

"Oh..." Flower shrinks back slightly, unsure how to read the deadpan delivery. "Sorry to have bothered you..."

The revenant blinks at Flower, caught off guard by the apology. For a split second her lips part as if to ask why, before she closes them and turns away.

choice("Try to explain to Flower that the revenant wasn't bothered by her", B2-Ch11c-Explain, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Explain, v_b2_ch11_explain = 1)
choice("Let them sort their problems by themselves", B2-Ch11c-Explain, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Explain, v_b2_ch11_explain = 0)


ID: B2-Ch11c-Explain
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch11_explain == 1) {
 "Hey, Flower," I say quietly.

"Yeah?"

"The revenant wasn't annoyed with you," I say. "That flat delivery is just how the undead speak. Ignore the tone if you want to avoid misunderstandings."

"Oh!" Flower brightens, turning back to the revenant. "Sorry! I thought you were mad at me for asking stupid questions. I didn't mean to upset you!"

"You did not upset me," the revenant says. "And your questions were not stupid."

"Glad to hear it!" Flower beams. She takes two steps forward and extends an open hand. "Friends?"

"I... already told you that if the God of Death orders your death, I will--"

"Doesn't matter!" Flower says cheerfully. "We can still be friends until then. If you want to, of course."

The revenant hesitates, staring down at Flower's small hand. Slowly, her fingers close around it in a firm, steady grip.

"Great!" Flower says. "Friends look out for each other. If you ever need a hand, just call. Unless you're trying to kill me, obviously."

"Thank you," the revenant says quietly. "I will remember that."

Flower skips over to join Melindra by the oak, while the rest of us wait in the quiet clearing. Daren and Hadrik circle the frozen treeline, testing the wards.

Leila stands well apart from the rest of us at the clearing's edge. She hasn't uttered a written word or moved from her spot since the trials ended, eyes glued to the dirt.

Her trial must have hit just as hard as Kate's. She grew up in the Beacon too, which means her loop probably dragged her through the same nightmare. Kate has years of built-up armor to weather that kind of trauma, but Leila carries hers raw.

}
#if(v_b2_ch11_explain == 0) {
 I stay out of it and let them work through the exchange on their own.

Flower moves over to join Melindra by the oak, while the rest of us wait in the quiet clearing. Daren and Hadrik circle the frozen treeline, testing the wards.

Leila stands well apart from the rest of us at the clearing's edge. She hasn't uttered a written word or moved from her spot since the trials ended, eyes glued to the dirt.

Her trial must have hit just as hard as Kate's. She grew up in the Beacon too, which means her loop probably dragged her through the same nightmare. Kate has years of built-up armor to weather that kind of trauma, but Leila carries hers raw.

}
 I walk over to her. "Leila, are you alright?"

She starts at my voice, looking up in surprise. She's far too used to fading into the background.

She meets my eyes and gives a small, careful nod.

"I'm fine," glowing blue letters form in the air before her. "Just a little shaken."

"Do you want to talk about it?"

Leila hesitates, then offers a faint, fragile smile as new words hang between us.

"Not now. But maybe later..."

I give Leila some space to collect herself and walk back toward the group.

As I return, I catch the revenant watching Kate, a faint tremor of hesitation in her posture.

"I have a question," the revenant says, her voice unusually subdued. "What happened to Kevin and Suzie? Were they sent to an orphanage, or--"

"No," Kate says gently. "We left them with Enrique. He moved into the house after hearing of Rose's death. He's looking after them."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11c-Anxiousness, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Anxiousness)


ID: B2-Ch11c-Anxiousness
TEXT:

#if(v_ch4_spared_dave == 1) {
 "Enrique..." the revenant murmurs, looking past Kate as if sifting through distant memories. "Yes. Enrique is a good man. He will protect the children. Thank you."

The tension in her posture eases into visible relief. The warmth is fleeting; within seconds, her features settle back into cold, expressionless stone.

"It's been a while since the first trials wrapped up," Daren notes, pacing back in from the trees. "Why is the second stage taking so long to trigger?"

"Because you idiots fed Selkram a feast," Arraka says. "He's still busy refining the haul into raw power."

"What haul?" Daren asks.

"Your misery!" Arraka snaps. "Negative emotion is the raw fuel Selkram harvests to convert into magical energy. While you losers were busy looping through your worst nightmares, drowning in despair and screaming curses at the sky, you filled his reserves to the brim. Honestly, you gave him so much juice he might not even need a second stage from us. We'll find out soon enough."

"Glad to be of service," Daren says dryly. "That gives us more time to search the perimeter. Come on, Hadrik. Let's test more of these frozen insects and branches. Maybe one isn't wrapped in an impenetrable ward, and we can crack this stasis from the outside."

Daren and Hadrik resume their sweep, but before they make it thirty paces, a shrill, panicked shriek tears through the trees a hundred feet away.

We whip around toward the commotion. Cutthroat Dave is stumbling backward through the underbrush, drenched in sweat, eyes darting in blind terror as he tries to make sense of the frozen woods. The invisible stalker Arraka and Selkram mentioned earlier—with everything that went down during the prophecy, I had completely forgotten about him.

"Well, look who finally dropped his cover," Melindra says with a smirk.

She flicks her wrist, and a spiraling updraft catches Dave by the belt, hoisting him kicking into the air.

"Why don't you join the party so we can get a proper look at you?"

The wind drags him toward us in steady increments while he thrashes against the invisible grip.

"Put me down, you witch!" Dave roars, hurling three throwing daggers straight at her face.

Melindra raises a casual gust that knocks the blades aside mid-flight, reeling him in without missing a beat.

}
#if(v_ch4_spared_dave == 0) {
 "Enrique..." the revenant murmurs, looking past Kate as if sifting through distant memories. "Yes. Enrique is a good man. He will protect the children. Thank you."

The tension in her posture eases into visible relief. The warmth is fleeting; within seconds, her features settle back into cold, expressionless stone.

"It's been a while since the first trials wrapped up," Daren notes, pacing back in from the trees. "Why is the second stage taking so long to trigger?"

"Because you idiots fed Selkram a feast," Arraka says. "He's still busy refining the haul into raw power."

"What haul?" Daren asks.

"Your misery!" Arraka snaps. "Negative emotion is the raw fuel Selkram harvests to convert into magical energy. While you losers were busy looping through your worst nightmares, drowning in despair and screaming curses at the sky, you filled his reserves to the brim. Honestly, you gave him so much juice he might not even need a second stage from us. We'll find out soon enough."

"Glad to be of service," Daren says dryly. "That gives us more time to search the perimeter. Come on, Hadrik. Let's test more of these frozen insects and branches. Maybe one isn't wrapped in an impenetrable ward, and we can crack this stasis from the outside."

Daren and Hadrik spend the next few minutes scouring the frozen foliage, but find nothing that yields to force.

A blinding white flash washes over the clearing. The pull hits my physical frame this time—not just my consciousness, but flesh and bone wrenched across space.

When the glare recedes, the forest is gone. We stand in the center of an impossible metropolis.

Four colossal black towers dominate each corner of the city skyline, their sheer surfaces gleaming with the matte dark sheen of seredium dust. Atop each spire, a violent vortex of purple magical energy churns wildly against invisible containment, bright enough to sear the eyes. Massive platforms glide up and down the flanks of the thousand-foot towers, shuttling hundreds of people between elevated levels.

Above our heads, hundreds of horseless metal carriages streak through the sky in frantic transit. Towering holographic screens hover over the avenues, projecting the looping broadcast of an announcer shouting in Common: 'All non-lessathi citizens are to present themselves to the military reserve area immediately. We are under attack. I repeat, we are under attack!'

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch11c-Jewelry, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Jewelry) if (v_ch4_spared_dave == 1)
choice("Continue", B2-Ch11c-Frenzy, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Frenzy) if (v_ch4_spared_dave == 0)


ID: B2-Ch11c-Jewelry
TEXT:

 "Watch out!" Daren shouts. "His daggers are animated! They track on their own even if you parry them!"

"Terrifying," Melindra says dryly. "Whatever will I do?"

She sweeps her hand through the air, and small, spinning wind tunnels enclose each dagger, freezing the blades in place mid-flight. Dave thrashes against the air currents holding him aloft, kicking furiously until Melindra drags him within arm's reach.

"What's all the shouting about, cutthroat?" Melindra asks. "Panic and break your own invisibility? Or was your trial so horrifying you're still jumping at your own shadow? How does a gutter-rat like you get his hands on high-grade concealment alchemy anyway?"

"Like I'd tell you where I bought the brew," Dave spits. "I'm way too smart for your interrogation games."

"So it was an elixir," Melindra says with a smirk. "Fascinating."

"He carried an expensive single-use teleportation scroll when we fought him in the ruins," Daren says. "I wouldn't be surprised if he has other contraband on him."

"Let's see what else he's hoarding."

Melindra flicks her fingers. A corked glass vial filled with green liquid and two furled parchment scrolls glide straight out of Dave's pockets into her waiting hands.

"Thieving witch!" Dave roars, straining against the updraft. "Give those back or I'll carve you into ribbons!"

"You'll carve me?" Melindra looks at the scrolls, her smile razor-thin. "Do you genuinely not understand who is holding you right now? Or did tournament fever burn out your last two brain cells?"

"You think I'm scared of a stillwater?" Dave sneers. "Your tin-plated friend was supposed to be a living legend too, and I brought him to his knees. You aren't special."

"Wait," Arraka's voice booms from the amulet. "The healer got dropped by this clown? Ahahahahaha! That is the funniest thing I've heard all century!"

"Who are you calling a clown, you cheap piece of jewelry?!" Dave barks.

"He called me jewelry!" Arraka shrieks with laughter. "Ahahahahaha! Somebody shut his mouth before my lungs burst!"

choice("Ask Melindra if I can have Cutthroat Dave's items.", B2-Ch11c-Items, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Items, v_b2_ch11_items = 1)
choice("Let Melindra do whatever she wants with the items.", B2-Ch11c-Items, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Items, v_b2_ch11_items = 0)


ID: B2-Ch11c-Items
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b2_ch11_elephant,1) if (v_ch3_kate_badly_hurt == 1)
achievement("The elephant in the room",v_ac_b2_ch11_elephant)
#if(v_b2_ch11_items == 1) {
 "Hey, Melindra," I say. "Can I have those?"

"Help yourself," Melindra says, tossing the vial and scrolls into my hands.

She handed them over without a second thought, which immediately puts me on guard. I unfurl the parchment and inspect the script. As suspected, the incantations are bound to Dave's personal magical frequency—useless to anyone else. The green elixir is likely tailored the same way.

Melindra catches my expression and smirks. "Dead weight, aren't they? The elixir might work on an uncalibrated frame, but I wouldn't drink black-market brew unless you fancy turning your spleen inside out."

"Better in my pack than in his hands," I say.

I slide the flask into my bag and rip the scrolls to shreds so Dave can't retrieve them.

}
#if(v_b2_ch11_items == 0) {
 "What are you going to do with those?" I ask Melindra.

"You want them?" she asks.

"Not particularly."

"Then this."

Melindra tears both scrolls down the middle and unplugs the flask, dumping the green liquid into the dirt while smiling at Dave as he shakes with rage.

"What did you do that for?" Daren protests. "We could have used those scrolls to reach Ollendor!"

"No, you couldn't," Melindra says. "They were keyed specifically to his signature. And the elixir was likely brewed for his biology. They were useless to us."

"Oh," Daren mutters. "Better destroyed, then."

}
 "You're all dead meat!" Dave snarls, glaring at us. "You think you can rob Cutthroat Dave? You're looking at the future champion of this tournament! You have no idea who you're crossing!"

"The future champion?" Arraka cackles. "Barry-boy, looks like you've finally met your match!"

"Shut up, Arraka," I mutter.

"I've heard enough," Kate says.

Three jagged ice cones form around her hands and rocket toward Dave's face.

Dave reacts on instinct, whipping three belt knives upward to shatter the icicles inches from his nose.

#if(v_ch3_kate_badly_hurt == 1) {
 "Jumping a defenseless man—" Dave pants, sweat beading on his forehead. "What the hell is wrong with your face? That is one grotesque burn scar. Did you lose a staring contest with a dragon?"

Kate's eyes go dead and flat. Five more icicles materialize above her shoulders, sharp as glass.

}
#if(v_ch3_kate_badly_hurt == 0) {
 "Jumping a defenseless man—" Dave pants, sweat beading on his forehead. "Is that how you fight? How about you put me down and face me like a proper combatant instead of a pack of alley thugs?"

Kate ignores him entirely. Five more icicles materialize above her shoulders, sharp as glass.

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch11c-Unleashes, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Unleashes)


ID: B2-Ch11c-Unleashes
TEXT:

#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
 Before Dave can draw another breath, Kate unleashes the volley. Two heavy ice cones punch clean through his forehead, killing him instantly.

"So much for the future champion," Melindra says, letting the updraft collapse to dump his corpse into the dirt.

"Good riddance," Hadrik grunts. "Wasted enough daylight on him. Let's get back to work."

The clearing falls quiet as we regroup, but moments later, blinding white light floods the forest. The pull grips my physical frame—not a psychic tug, but flesh, blood, and gear ripped across space.

When the glare recedes, the forest is gone. We stand in the center of an impossible metropolis.

Four colossal black towers dominate each corner of the city skyline, their sheer surfaces gleaming with the matte dark sheen of seredium dust. Atop each spire, a violent vortex of purple magical energy churns wildly against invisible containment, bright enough to sear the eyes. Massive platforms glide up and down the flanks of the thousand-foot towers, shuttling hundreds of people between elevated levels.

Above our heads, hundreds of horseless metal carriages streak through the sky in frantic transit. Towering holographic screens hover over the avenues, projecting the looping broadcast of an announcer shouting in Common: 'All non-lessathi citizens are to present themselves to the military reserve area immediately. We are under attack. I repeat, we are under attack!'

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
 "Wait," the revenant says, stepping forward. "Do not kill him."

"Give me one reason why," Kate says, ice still hovering at her fingertips.

"He may serve a purpose in the trial ahead," the revenant says. "Let me question him."

"Ask," Kate says, "but I make no promises about leaving him breathing. He is just as likely to be a liability."

The revenant steps directly into Dave's line of sight, meeting his frantic gaze.

"Why were you trailing this group under concealment? Who ordered you to track them?"

"Cutthroat Dave answers to no one!" Dave barks, spitting into the wind. "I followed you because I couldn't get close enough to slit your throats in your sleep this morning!"

"Why could you not approach?"

"Because the damn elixir didn't mask my aura properly!" Dave snaps. "You had sentries posted around the tents. Since the potion was already burned, I tailed you waiting for your guard to drop!"

"How did you locate our campsite initially?"

"Are you deaf? Pure accident! Nobody pointed the way. Now tell your witch friend to drop me so we can settle this properly--"

A blinding white flash swallows the clearing mid-sentence. The pull grips my physical frame—not a psychic tug, but flesh, blood, and gear ripped across space.

When the glare recedes, the forest is gone. We stand in the center of an impossible metropolis.

Four colossal black towers dominate each corner of the city skyline, their sheer surfaces gleaming with the matte dark sheen of seredium dust. Atop each spire, a violent vortex of purple magical energy churns wildly against invisible containment, bright enough to sear the eyes. Massive platforms glide up and down the flanks of the thousand-foot towers, shuttling hundreds of people between elevated levels.

Above our heads, hundreds of horseless metal carriages streak through the sky in frantic transit. Towering holographic screens hover over the avenues, projecting the looping broadcast of an announcer shouting in Common: 'All non-lessathi citizens are to present themselves to the military reserve area immediately. We are under attack. I repeat, we are under attack!'

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch11c-Frenzy, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Frenzy)


ID: B2-Ch11c-Frenzy
TEXT:

