choice("Load game", , , special:saves) if (v_b2_ch11_dagger == 1 && v_b2_ch11_echoes == 0)


ID: B2-Ch11c-Avalanche
TEXT:

 A roaring cascade of magma-flame crashes into past-Arraka from the right alleyway, heat so intense the stones beneath our boots crack and weep liquid glass.

Past-Arraka catches the brunt across a hasty barrier, skidding backward under the kinetic fury of the firestorm as the street liquefies into churning lava. Pressed to the brink, she teleports fifty feet straight up into the smoky air, and the torrent cuts off.

Beside us, the magma dragon Tyrath materializes in a swirl of sulfur and radiant heat.

Before we can brace, he snaps his wings forward and teleports our entire group away from the burning street.

We drop onto solid stone inside a colossal amphitheater. Every wall and arching ceiling beam is coated in the matte black texture of seredium dust. Tiered rows of seating rise into the shadows, illuminated by crystalline devices embedded in the railings.

Hundreds of Lessathi councilors in ornate robes murmur among themselves in the tiers. In the central floor where we stand, a dozen human casters and women in traditional wizard robes surround an elevated dais.

An ancient Lessathi with a long white beard addresses Tyrath from the platform, his voice polite and drenched in condescension.

"Who are these bizarrely clad vagabonds you drag before the Lessathi High Council, King of Dragons? We agreed to a closed-door session. An audience does not extend to uninvited guests."

"I did not ask your permission, old fool," Tyrath rumbles, steam hissing from his scales. "I would not set foot in your hall if the calamity were not absolute. This outworld wizard Arraka is an existential threat. My entire dragon flight fell before her yesterday. Only I possessed the power to check her advance, and even I cannot destroy her. You have seen her handiwork across your skies."

"If the crisis is grave, why burden this chamber with stragglers?" Ezzeloff asks. "What utility could they possibly offer?"

"They are not common stragglers," Tyrath says. "I overheard Arraka name one of them a time weaver. And the aura radiating from the trinket at that child's neck mirrors Arraka's own signature. The creature was seconds from executing them. Even your narrow council might grasp the value of allies who share her weaknesses."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11c-Ezzeloff, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Ezzeloff)


ID: B2-Ch11c-Ezzeloff
TEXT:

 "At what cost?!" another Lessathi shouts, rising from the tiered benches. "Word from my sentries confirms your firestorm incinerated over a hundred civilians in that thoroughfare! Was it worth slaughtering our own people to preserve this handful?"

"Had my fire not fallen, Arraka would have butchered them seconds later," Tyrath snarls. "Your civilians were corpses the instant she entered the district. Hundreds of your aerial carriages had already crashed before I struck. Every heartbeat wasted arguing here costs another hundred lives until we forge a viable plan of attack."

"Tyrath is correct that bickering will not serve us," Ezzeloff says. "Be seated, Councilman Hrelezar. We will interrogate these strangers shortly and determine whether their survival justified the loss."

Hrelezar sits back down, scowling. Ezzeloff turns to one of the robed women.

"Olyrra, continue your report."

"As I stated, Chairman Ezzeloff," the woman says, "the phenomenon is unprecedented. Yesterday, our magical power vanished completely. Only female casters are affected, but even our highest-tier combat wizards have been reduced to commoners overnight. No stimulant or focal ritual restores the flow. Without a remedy, half our elite divisions are useless in the coming battle."

"A grim reality," Ezzeloff says. "Restoring their magic must remain our highest priority."

"Keep dreaming, councilman," Arraka's voice crackles from the amulet. "Your women are never casting another spell."

"What are you talking about?" Ezzeloff demands.

"You didn't find it curious that every woman on this continent lost her magic the exact day my past self breached this realm?" Arraka sneers. "Of course you didn't. You're morons. Female casters in this era drew power by subconsciously tethering themselves to female spirits on the magical plane. Yesterday was the day I and every other female spirit were permanently exiled from that realm. The bridge is broken. Hand them swords and shields—it's the only combat utility you'll get from them now."

"You referred to the attacker as a past version of yourself..." Tyrath rumbles. "You originate from the future?"

"Brilliant deduction, Tyrath!" Arraka says. "I knew basic logic would click for you eventually. No wonder the other reptiles made you king."

"Spare me your venom and answer clearly," Tyrath says. "Why have you come? Why were you battling the Arraka of our era? Did you travel back to avert a catastrophe, or did you stumble here by chance?"

choice(""Actually, we were sent here against our own will."", B2-Ch11c-Against, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Against)
choice(""We came here to save you all from this great threat!"", B2-Ch11c-Threat, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Threat)
choice(""Sorry, we only came here on vacation."", B2-Ch11c-Vacation, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Vacation)


ID: B2-Ch11c-Against
TEXT:

 "Actually, we were sent here against our own will."

"Explain," Tyrath says.

"Do you know anything about the God of Time and his rituals?" I ask.

"I have no concern for gods or their diversions," Tyrath says. "Am I to understand this 'God of Time' hurled you across eras?"

"Exactly," I say.

"And you seek a means of return?"

"Our immediate priority is simply staying alive," I say.

"Then cooperation offers your highest probability of survival," Tyrath says. "Share what you know of the future, and we are far better equipped to destroy Arraka."

"What kind of information are you looking for?" I ask.

"I have drafted a strategy that represents our sole viable path to neutralizing Arraka permanently," Tyrath says. "I have coordinated with the High Council's battle mages since yesterday, after I warned them of her advance. Given that you hail from the future, confirm whether this maneuver was enacted in your past and whether it held. Nearby lies a mountain range we can use to--"

"Let me stop you right there, Tyrath," Arraka's voice crackles from the amulet. "The good news is that in our past, your plan worked: you managed to seal me inside that mountain for several thousand years. The bad news is that I just told the Arraka of your era every detail of the trap, which means your strategy is completely useless now."

"You did WHAT?!" Tyrath roars, the magma along his crest detonating in blinding geysers.

Before Arraka can gloat further, a blinding white light engulfs the amphitheater. The pull snaps us backward through reality, depositing us back into the frozen forest.

Overhead, the God of Time hovers thirty feet above the grass, his faceless silhouette radiating quiet malice.

"I am afraid our session must conclude for now," the God of Time speaks, his voice echoing in layered resonance. "I have reached the maximum energy extraction threshold permissible from a single cohort. Do not fret; you will resume this trial when the time weaver delivers her next cycle. You exceeded my expectations. You have my sincere commendations."

"Hah!" Arraka sneers. "Told you losers you gave him everything he--"

"And your contribution outshone them all, Arraka," Selkram cuts her off.

"What did you say?"

"I had not dared hope you would participate willingly," Selkram says, cold amusement rippling through his tone. "Yet your exquisite terror of total erasure by the Magium yielded the richest harvest of the entire ritual. I eagerly anticipate your performance in the trials to come."

"You miserable bastard--" Arraka snarls.

"Now, before I take my leave, you will kneel before me one final time," the God of Time commands.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11c-Dave, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Dave) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 0 && v_ch4_spared_dave == 1)
choice("Continue", B2-Ch11c-Forehead, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Forehead) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 1 || v_ch4_spared_dave == 0)


ID: B2-Ch11c-Threat
TEXT:

 "We came here to save you all from this great threat!"

"Bold words from mortals I pulled from the jaws of incineration," Tyrath rumbles. "Provide actionable intelligence from the future rather than hollow bravado."

"What kind of information are you looking for?" I ask.

"I have drafted a strategy that represents our sole viable path to neutralizing Arraka permanently," Tyrath says. "I have coordinated with the High Council's battle mages since yesterday, after I warned them of her advance. Given that you hail from the future, confirm whether this maneuver was enacted in your past and whether it held. Nearby lies a mountain range we can use to--"

