ID: B2-Ch11c-Dave
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b2_ch11_cutthroat,1)
achievement("Cutthroat vs god",v_ac_b2_ch11_cutthroat)
 "Kneel?" Dave's voice cuts in, making us realize he was pulled back into the clearing alongside us. "Kneel?!"

He stalks to the front, craning his neck up at the floating entity.

"Cutthroat Dave kneels for no one."

"You will bow before your god, or face the same disassembly the others endured when they defied me," the God of Time speaks.

Dave spits into the dirt in disgust.

"You're no god. All you are is an ugly, faceless bastard with an overinflated ego. You're just like every arrogant caster in this tournament—flashing magic around and expecting the world to bend. Earn your respect instead of acting like it's your birthright. You can pretend you're special all you want, but deep down you'll always know what you really are: a worthless piece of sh--"

Dave vanishes mid-curse. A thin coil of gray smoke drifts upward from the patch of grass where he stood.

"What did you do to him?..." Kate asks, stepping forward.

"He will spend additional time in the era of your previous trial as recompense for his insolence," the God of Time says. "If he survives the interim, you will meet him when you resume your ritual. Now. I will not repeat myself. Kneel."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11c-Forehead, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Forehead)


ID: B2-Ch11c-Forehead
TEXT:

#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
 We exchange tight glances across the grass. Not a single person drops to a knee.

Melindra closes her eyes and slowly rubs her temples in exasperation. Knowing the outcome is fixed either way, she plants her feet beside us.

"Have it your way," the God of Time says.

An invisible weight slams us into the dirt, flattening everyone except the revenant. The pressure steadily intensifies, grinding bone and breath into the soil.

Pressed against the roots, the cabin rushes back through my mind. Helen and Jonathan screaming for help while I stand paralyzed in the doorway, pitchfork locked in trembling hands.

A cold fury hardens in my chest. With every ounce of pressure grinding my ribs, a single vow repeats behind my teeth.

'I will make him pay.'

'I will make him pay.'

'I will make him pay.'

While the spell grinds us down, the revenant's eyes ignite with bright yellow light. She turns and walks into the trees, her blank stare fixed on the horizon.

"Our time concludes," Selkram's voice booms from above. "I shall see you again shortly. Endeavor to survive until then. Your future contributions are anticipated."

Selkram and the revenant vanish together. The world snaps back into motion—leaves rustle, insects hum, the pressure lifts.

We push ourselves up from the dirt in silence, brushing the soil from our clothes.

My transceiver clicks in my pocket. The revenant's voice comes through, low and guarded.

"What transpired after my departure? Did the God of Time linger?"

"No," I say, tapping the stone. "From our perspective, you vanished seconds ago. What about you? Remember anything from the walk?"

"Mostly a blur," she answers. "I remember my next recipients were Azarius and his apprentice, but nothing beyond that."

"Are you keeping your transceiver channel closed?"

"Yes. I am still under tight surveillance. If an urgent need arises, I will initiate contact. Stay alive."

"You too," I say, and the link clicks dead.

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
 We exchange tight glances across the grass. Not a single person drops to a knee.

Melindra closes her eyes and slowly rubs her temples in exasperation. Knowing the outcome is fixed either way, she plants her feet beside us.

"Have it your way," the God of Time says.

An invisible weight slams us into the dirt, flattening everyone except Rose. The pressure steadily intensifies, grinding bone and breath into the soil.

Pressed against the roots, the cabin rushes back through my mind. Helen and Jonathan screaming for help while I stand paralyzed in the doorway, pitchfork locked in trembling hands.

A cold fury hardens in my chest. With every ounce of pressure grinding my ribs, a single vow repeats behind my teeth.

'I will make him pay.'

'I will make him pay.'

'I will make him pay.'

While the spell grinds us down, Rose's eyes ignite with bright yellow light. She turns and walks into the trees, her blank stare fixed on the horizon.

"Our time concludes," Selkram's voice booms from above. "I shall see you again shortly. Endeavor to survive until then. Your future contributions are anticipated."

Selkram and Rose vanish together. The world snaps back into motion—leaves rustle, insects hum, the pressure lifts.

We push ourselves up from the dirt in silence, brushing the soil from our clothes.

My transceiver clicks in my pocket. Rose's voice comes through, warm and worried.

"Is everyone alright? Did anything happen after I left?"

"We're fine," I say, tapping the transceiver. "From our perspective, it's only been a few seconds. Do you remember the walk?"

"Mostly a blur," Rose says. "I remember visiting Azarius and Henry right after you, but the rest is hazy."

"If anything strange happens on your end, call us."

"I will. I need to check on the children now. We'll talk soon."

"Talk soon," I say, closing the link.

}
 "We need a break," Daren says, looking hollowed out with fatigue. "Shall we meet back here in twenty minutes?"

"Yeah," Hadrik grunts. "I could use twenty minutes."

The group drifts apart into the trees. Daren sits on a mossy stump, drawing a whetstone across his blade in rhythmic, unnecessary strokes to keep his hands busy.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11c-Ending, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Ending)


ID: B2-Ch11c-Ending
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b2_ch11_average2,1) if (v_available_points == 25)
achievement("Average Joe 2",v_ac_b2_ch11_average2)
 "So..." I say, walking over to Daren's stump. "What's the plan once the twenty minutes are up?"

"What do you mean?" Daren keeps his eyes on the steel, stone grinding steadily against the edge. "We march toward Ollendor. That was the objective when we left Thilias, wasn't it?"

"You know that's not what I'm asking," I say. "I'm talking about the God of Time. The upcoming trials hanging over our heads. What do we do about him?"

Daren stops the whetstone and looks up, his dark eyes tired. "You tell me, Barry. I don't see an easy exit. We train, we prepare for the next stage, and we hope we survive it like we survived the first."

"I was thinking of a more permanent solution," I say.

Daren frowns. "Like what? Killing the God of Time? Barry, be realistic. We're talking about an actual deity. We nearly broke against a sage a few days ago. What do you expect us to do against a god? We aren't remotely strong enough."

"Funny," I say. "You aren't remotely as strong as Eiden either, and you challenge him every time he shows his face."

"That's different!" Daren snaps. "Eiden is a mage. We understand mages. Some are stronger, some have terrifying tricks, but the rules are the same. A god is fundamentally different. Neither of us has a shred of experience dealing with something on that scale."

"Then we ask someone who does," I say.

I turn on my heel before Daren can answer and walk toward Illuna, who is in a quiet conversation with the amulet.

They stop talking as I approach, watching me.

"Arraka," I say, keeping my voice level and deadly serious. "I have a question for you."

"Sure," Arraka's voice comes back, mildly amused. "Fire away."

"How do you kill a god?"

Silence settles over the clearing.

"You want to know how to kill a god?" Arraka asks, her tone completely flat, as if processing the words one by one.

She waits for a few seconds, looking for the punchline, waiting for me to blink or crack a grin.

When she realizes I am completely serious, the laughter begins.

It starts low and builds into a deafening, three-voiced roar that shakes the trees around us. It isn't her usual cruel mockery. It's raw, unhinged excitement.

The laughter pulls the rest of the group back toward the clearing, their eyes wide as they stare at us. I ignore them, standing in front of the gold chain until the storm finally ebbs.

The cackling tapers off into ragged, gleeful chuckles. When she finally speaks, her voice is purring with delight.

"Barry, my boy..." Arraka says. "I thought you'd never ask!"

-----End of book 2-----

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11c-Credits, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Credits, v_second_book_purchased = 1)


ID: B2-Ch11c-Credits
TEXT:

set(v_iap_check_resume,1) if (v_iap_checked != 0 && v_iap_checked != 2)
set(v_iap_check_resume,0)
set(v_second_book_purchased,1)
 This marks the end of the second Magium book.

Only the first eleven chapters of the 3rd book are available so far, but the rest of the chapters will be released one by one, as I write them. (the main series will likely have 5 books in total)

My current estimate is that the 12th chapter of the third book should be released within the next few months.

If you already paid to unlock the 3rd book, you can tap on the button below to proceed. Otherwise, you will need to complete 25 achievements from the second book in order to unlock the third one.

You can see how many more achievements you need to complete in the achievements menu. 

I would also like to add that I strongly suggest you save the game now on a save slot you won't overwrite, if you're planning to start completing achievements, since otherwise you'll lose the progress of your current playthrough and you'll have to finish the game again in order to be able to progress to the next book.

You should also be careful to not uninstall the game, since all of the next books will be uploaded within the same app, and if you uninstall the game, all your local progress, including your achievements, will be lost.

Thank you for playing!

P.S. If you enjoyed the game, don't forget to recommend it to your friends. 

choice("Proceed to book 3", B3-Ch01a-Intro, v_b2_saved_stats = 1, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Intro, v_chapter_save_counter = 5, v_checkpoint_rich = 1, special:checkpoint_save)
choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves)
